s University, Baltimore, MD, USA.\nL188:   * ^{3} Sheffield Institute for Translational Neuroscience, University of Sheffield, Sheffield, UK; National Institute for Health and Care Research Sheffield Biomedical Research Centre and Clinical Research Facility, University of Sheffield and Sheffield Teaching Hospitals NHS Foundation Trust, Sheffield, UK.\nL189:   * ^{4} Sean M Healey and AMG Center for ALS, Department of Neurology, Massachusetts General Hospital, Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA, USA.\nL190:   * ^{5} Department of Neurology, University of Miami Miller School of Medicine, Miami, FL, USA.\nL191:   * ^{6} Department of Neurology, Washington University School of Medicine in St Louis, St Louis, MO, USA.\nL192:   * ^{7} Montreal Neurological Institute and Hospital, Montreal, Canada.\nL193:   * ^{8} Department of Neurology, Emory University, Atlanta, GA, USA.\nL194:   * ^{9} School of Medicine, Trinity College, Dublin, Ireland.\nL195:   * ^{10} University College London Institute of Neurology and National Institute for Health and Care Research (NIHR) University College London Hospitals (UCLH) Clinical Research Facility and Biomedical Research Centre, London, UK.\nL196:   * ^{11} Cumming School of Medicine, University of Calgary, Calgary, Alberta, Canada.\nL197:   * ^{12} Department of Neurology, Mayo Clinic, Jacksonville, FL, USA.\nL198:   * ^{13} Bryan Physicians Network, Lincoln, NE, USA; Department of Neurological Sciences, University of Nebraska Medical Center, Omaha, NE, USA.\nL199:   * ^{14} Department of Neurosciences, University of California San Diego Health, San Diego, CA, USA.\nL200:   * ^{15} UK Dementia Research Institute, King's College London, London, UK.\nL201:   * ^{16} Neuromuscular Diseases Unit/ALS Clinic, Kantonsspital St Gallen, Switzerland.\nL202:   * ^{17} Division of Neurology, Department of Medicine, Sunnybrook Health Sciences Centre, University of Toronto, Toronto, Canada.\nL203:   * ^{18} Ionis Pharmaceuticals, Carlsbad, CA, USA.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn92view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://investors.biogen.com/news-releases/news-release-details/biogen-and-ionis-announce-topline-phase-12-study-results\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://investors.biogen.com/news-releases/news-release-details/biogen-and-ionis-announce-topline-phase-12-study-results: (403) Forbidden--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUpdate on WVE-004 program in C9-ALS/FTD (https://wavelifesciences.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/12/C9-Community-Letter-5-23-23.pdf)\nciteturn92view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 2; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://wavelifesciences.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/12/C9-Community-Letter-5-23-23.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 55\nL0@P0: May 23, 2023\nL1@P0: Dear ALS and FTD Communities,\nL2@P0: We are sharing this update to keep you informed of Wave Life Sciences’ FOCUS-C9\nL3@P0: and FOCUS-C9 open label extension studies, which are Phase 1b/2a clinical trials\nL4@P0: investigating WVE-004 for people with C9orf72-associated amyotrophic lateral\nL5@P0: sclerosis (C9-ALS) and/or frontotemporal dementia (C9-FTD).\nL6@P0: Background\nL7@P0: WVE-004 is an investigational antisense oligonucleotide (ASO) designed to lower\nL8@P0: the unwanted, disease-causing proteins and preserve healthy C9orf72 protein in\nL9@P0: people with C9orf72-ALS and/or FTD. In April 2022, we shared data that WVE-004\nL10@P0: demonstrated reductions in poly(GP), a key disease biomarker. This indicated that\nL11@P0: WVE-004 was acting as intended, and the reduction was observed across all active\nL12@P0: treatment groups after a single dose. Based on these findings, we adapted the\nL13@P0: FOCUS-C9 study to expand the single dose groups to optimize dose level and\nL14@P0: frequency and extend the follow-up period to enable further assessments, and we\nL15@P0: also advanced two multidose groups.\nL16@P0: Data Update\nL17@P0: After reviewing data from the multidose phase of the study, we have made the\nL18@P0: difficult decision to discontinue development of WVE-004. This decision was based\nL19@P0: on the following:\nL20@P0: - We saw substantial reductions of poly(GP) with multiple doses.\nL21@P0: - However, we did not see any evidence of clinical benefit on outcome\nL22@P0: measures for ALS or FTD.\nL23@P0: - We also did not see any indication that poly(GP) was correlated with clinical\nL24@P0: outcomes.\nL25@P0: WVE-004 was generally safe and well-tolerated.\nL26@P0: - Most adverse events (AEs) presented as mild in intensity and the most\nL27@P0: common AEs were related to disease progression and administration.\nL28@P0: - There was no evidence of inflammation in the CSF as there were no clinically\nL29@P0: meaningful changes in CSF protein or white blood cell count.\nL30@P0: - Among WVE-004-treated participants, there was one SAE in the study\nL31@P0: reported by the investigator as related to study drug that occurred in the 60\nL32@P0: mg single dose cohort, as previously reported in April 2022. There was also\nL33@P0: one SAE reported that was procedure related. All other SAEs were associated\nL34@P0-1: with disease progression. 2\nL35@P1: We recognize that this news is difficult for the ALS and FTD communities and\nL36@P1: especially for those who took part in the study. On behalf of everyone at Wave, we\nL37@P1: want to sincerely thank the participants, their families, the clinical sites,\nL38@P1: investigators, and our clinical advisory committees who dedicated their time and\nL39@P1: effort over the past several years to advance these studies.\nL40@P1: While the FOCUS-C9 study and FOCUS-C9 open label extension study have stopped\nL41@P1: dosing, we recognize that there is additional work to complete the studies such as\nL42@P1: follow-up visits and assessments. For those who are currently participating in the\nL43@P1: FOCUS-C9 studies, we expect that you will be having a conversation with your\nL44@P1: study site investigator or staff in the coming days to provide more specific\nL45@P1: information on this. We are working closely with the FOCUS-C9 investigators and\nL46@P1: clinical site staff as we navigate next steps.\nL47@P1: We are deeply saddened to share this news and are truly grateful for the support\nL48@P1: from the ALS and FTD communities. Despite this outcome, the WVE-004 studies\nL49@P1: have resulted in a significant body of data which we are committed to sharing at an\nL50@P1: upcoming medical congress. It is our hope and belief that the learnings from this\nL51@P1: study can advance future research to benefit individuals with C9-ALS and C9-FTD.\nL52@P1: Sincerely,\nL53@P1: Chelley Casey, VP Patient Advocacy\nL54@P1: Wave Life Sciences\n"
